            {
              "end_index": 6716,
              "start_index": 6571,
              "title": "LEADER JEFFRIES STATEMENT ON DESIGNATION OF EMMETT TILL MEMORIAL – Congressman Hakeem Jeffries",
              "type": "url_citation",
              "url": "https://jeffries.house.gov/2023/07/25/leader-jeffries-statement-on-designation-of-emmett-till-memorial/?utm_source=openai"
            }
          ],
          "text": "1) Evidence that Hakeem Jeffries values human rights\n\nConclusion: Jeffries’ record as a U.S. Representative shows consistent support for core human-rights and civil-rights protections in the United States and abroad. This is evidenced by his yes votes for major rights legislation, his sponsorship of criminal-justice reform, and independent civil‑liberties ratings.\n\nKey evidence\n- LGBTQ+ equality and anti-discrimination: Voted yes on the Equality Act (H.R. 5) on February 25, 2021, a comprehensive civil-rights bill adding sexual orientation and gender identity to federal anti-discrimination laws. House roll call confirms passage 224–206; Vote Smart records Jeffries’ Yes vote and co‑sponsorship. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202139?utm_source=openai))\n- Making lynching a federal hate crime: Voted yes on the Emmett Till Antilynching Act (H.R. 55) on February 28, 2022 (House passage 422–3). Official House roll-call record for H.R. 55 documents the vote. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202247?utm_source=openai))\n- Policing and civil-rights accountability: Voted yes on the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act (H.R. 1280) on March 3, 2021 (passed 220–212), aimed at banning chokeholds and strengthening accountability. Official roll call confirms passage. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202160?utm_source=openai))\n- Protecting voting rights: Voted yes on the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act (H.R. 4) on August 24, 2021 (passed 219–212), to restore and update Voting Rights Act protections. Official House roll-call record confirms passage; Jeffries also publicly urged Senate action to overcome the filibuster to enact voting rights. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2021260?utm_source=openai))\n- Combating forced labor and mass abuses in Xinjiang: Voted yes to pass the Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act (H.R. 1155) on December 8, 2021 (428–1), to keep products made with forced labor out of U.S. supply chains. The House Clerk shows the roll‑call result; Congress.gov shows the bill’s actions. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2021412?utm_source=openai))\n- Advancing global LGBTQI+ human rights: The House passed the Global Respect Act (H.R. 3485) on February 9, 2022 (227–206) to sanction foreign perpetrators of anti‑LGBTQI human-rights abuses; Congress.gov details the bill’s rights purpose. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202243?utm_source=openai))\n- Ending forced arbitration for sexual assault/harassment: Voted yes on the Ending Forced Arbitration of Sexual Assault and Sexual Harassment Act (H.R. 4445) on February 7, 2022 (335–97), expanding survivors’ access to court. Official roll call and law summary confirm. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202233?utm_source=openai))\n- Criminal-justice reform leadership: Primary sponsor of the EQUAL Act (H.R. 1693) to eliminate the federal crack/powder cocaine sentencing disparity; the House passed it 361–66 on September 28, 2021. Congress.gov lists Jeffries as sponsor and the successful House vote. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1693/actions?utm_source=openai))\n- Independent civil‑liberties assessment: The ACLU’s 117th Congress scorecard shows Jeffries voted with ACLU civil‑liberties positions 100% of the time measured, indicating strong alignment with protecting rights and liberties. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/ny-hakeem-jeffries/?utm_source=openai))\n- Expressed values consistent with human-rights protection: Jeffries’ office publicly lauded the historic anti‑lynching law and the Emmett Till and Mamie Till‑Mobley National Monument, underscoring the moral imperative of confronting racist violence. ([jeffries.house.gov](https://jeffries.house.gov/2023/07/25/leader-jeffries-statement-on-designation-of-emmett-till-memorial/?utm_source=openai))\n\nTaken together—affirmative votes on landmark civil-rights and human-rights bills, authorship of criminal-justice reform, and third‑party civil‑liberties ratings—provide strong, objective evidence that Hakeem Jeffries values human rights.\n\n\n2) Trustworthy sources considered (and why)\n\n- Source: Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives\n  - Reasoning 1: The Clerk maintains the authoritative, official roll‑call vote records for the House; it is a primary U.S. government source. ([clerk.house.gov](https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/202139?utm_source=openai))\n  - Reasoning 2: For this prompt, the Clerk’s pages directly document whether and how Jeffries voted on each bill, which is the most reliable way to establish his record.\n\n- Source: Congress.gov (Library of Congress)\n  - Reasoning 1: Congress.gov is the official legislative information system managed by the Library of Congress, widely regarded as the definitive repository for bill texts, histories, and sponsorship. ([congress.gov](https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/1693/actions?utm_source=openai))\n  - Reasoning 2: It confirms bill purposes (e.g., Global Respect Act), sponsors (e.g., Jeffries on the EQUAL Act), and official actions, corroborating how the measures protect human rights.\n\n- Source: American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU) Congressional Scorecard\n  - Reasoning 1: The ACLU is a longstanding, nationally recognized civil‑liberties organization; its scorecards disclose methodology and track specific votes. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/?utm_source=openai))\n  - Reasoning 2: Jeffries’ 100% alignment in the 117th Congress gives an independent, issue‑focused view of his rights record beyond any single bill. ([aclu.org](https://www.aclu.org/scorecard/legislators/ny-hakeem-jeffries/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Source: Vote Smart\n  - Reasoning 1: Vote Smart is a nonpartisan, nonprofit project compiling public officials’ voting records with transparent selection criteria. ([justfacts.votesmart.org](https://j